---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`Forest and Wildlife Resources | CBSE Class 10 Geography | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 Geography Chapter 2 study notes on Forest and Wildlife Resources. Biodiversity, IUCN classification (normal, endangered, vulnerable, rare, endemic, extinct), causes of depletion, forest categories (reserved, protected, unclassed), community conservation (Chipko, Beej Bachao Andolan, JFM) with 20 board exam quiz questions."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Forest and Wildlife Resources
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · Geography Chapter 2</span>
      <h1>Forest and Wildlife Resources</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Explore India's rich biodiversity: IUCN species classification, factors driving ecological loss, state classification into Reserved, Protected, and Unclassed forests, and indigenous community conservation movements.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Flora, Fauna &amp; Conservation Framework</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌿 Biodiversity &amp; IUCN Species Classification</h3>
          <p class="formula">Species Classification (IUCN Categories)</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Biodiversity:</strong> The immense variety of plant and animal life in their natural habitats, mutually interdependent in complex ecological webs. India has over 81,000 species of fauna and 47,000 species of flora (10% of world's recorded biodiversity).</li>
            <li><strong>1. Normal Species:</strong> Populations considered normal for survival (cattle, sal, pine, rodents).</li>
            <li><strong>2. Endangered Species:</strong> In danger of extinction if negative factors continue (Black buck, crocodile, Indian wild ass, Indian rhino, lion-tailed macaque, sangai).</li>
            <li><strong>3. Vulnerable Species:</strong> Population has declined to levels from where it is likely to move into endangered category soon (Blue sheep, Asiatic elephant, Gangetic dolphin).</li>
            <li><strong>4. Rare Species:</strong> Small populations that may enter endangered/vulnerable if negative factors operate (Himalayan brown bear, wild Asiatic water buffalo, desert fox, hornbill).</li>
            <li><strong>5. Endemic Species:</strong> Found only in particular geographic areas isolated by natural barriers (Andaman teal, Nicobar pigeon, Andaman wild pig, Mithun in Arunachal).</li>
            <li><strong>6. Extinct Species:</strong> Not found after extensive searches in known habitats (Asiatic cheetah, Pink-headed duck).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚠️ Major Factors Causing Depletion</h3>
          <p class="formula">Colonial Roots, River Projects &amp; Mining</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Colonial Forestry:</strong> Commercial monoculture plantations (e.g. teak monoculture in South India) damaged natural mixed forest ecology.</li>
            <li><strong>Agricultural Expansion:</strong> Between 1951 and 1980, over 26,200 sq. km of forest area was converted into agricultural land across India. Shifting cultivation (Jhum) in Northeast and Central India.</li>
            <li><strong>Large-scale River Valley Projects:</strong> Since 1951, over 5,000 sq. km of forest cleared for dams (e.g. Narmada Sagar Project in MP submerged 4,000 sq. km of forest).</li>
            <li><strong>Mining:</strong> Open-cast mining (e.g. Buxa Tiger Reserve in West Bengal threatened by dolomite mining).</li>
            <li><strong>Other Drivers:</strong> Habitat destruction, poaching, over-grazing, fuel-wood collection, and forest fires.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🏛️ Forest Administration: 3 Categories</h3>
          <p class="formula">Government Classification of Forests</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>1. Reserved Forests:</strong> More than half of total forest land. Regarded as the most valuable as far as conservation of forest and wildlife is concerned. Strictly protected; no private rights (J&amp;K, Andhra, Uttarakhand, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, WB, Maharashtra).</li>
            <li><strong>2. Protected Forests:</strong> Almost one-third of total forest land. Protected from any further depletion; local communities often have limited rights to collect dry wood and graze cattle (Bihar, Haryana, Punjab, Himachal, Odisha, Rajasthan).</li>
            <li><em>Permanent Forest Estate:</em> Reserved + Protected forests maintained for producing timber and protective ecological reasons. (Madhya Pradesh has the largest area under permanent forests—75% of its forest area).</li>
            <li><strong>3. Unclassed Forests:</strong> Other forests and wastelands belonging to both government and private individuals/communities (bulk of Northeast states and parts of Gujarat).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>👥 Community Conservation &amp; JFM</h3>
          <p class="formula">Grassroots Ecological Protection</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Sariska Tiger Reserve (Rajasthan):</strong> Villagers fought against dolomite mining citing the Wildlife Protection Act.</li>
            <li><strong>Bhairondev Dakav 'Sanchuri':</strong> Villagers of five villages in Alwar district declared 1,200 hectares of forest as sanctuary, creating their own rules banning hunting and commercial encroachment.</li>
            <li><strong>Chipko Movement:</strong> In Himalayas (Uttarakhand), villagers hugged trees to resist commercial logging and revived indigenous afforestation.</li>
            <li><strong>Beej Bachao Andolan &amp; Navdanya:</strong> Farmers in Tehri proved that diverse crops can be grown without synthetic chemicals and are economically viable.</li>
            <li><strong>Joint Forest Management (JFM):</strong> Program launched in 1988 (first passed in Odisha). Involves local village institutions in restoring degraded forests. Communities get rights to non-timber forest products and share in timber harvested.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Species Vulnerability Spectrum &amp; Forest Classification</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 1 · The IUCN Extinction Risk Hierarchy</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="IUCN Species Status Flow">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- 4 Stages Spectrum -->
              <!-- Stage 1: Normal -->
              <rect x="25" y="80" width="95" height="90" fill="#dcfce7" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/>
              <text x="72" y="105" fill="#15803d" font-size="10.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">NORMAL</text>
              <text x="72" y="125" fill="#334155" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Sal, Pine, Cattle</text>
              <text x="72" y="145" fill="#166534" font-size="8" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Healthy numbers</text>

              <!-- Stage 2: Vulnerable -->
              <rect x="135" y="80" width="95" height="90" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/>
              <text x="182" y="105" fill="#b45309" font-size="10.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">VULNERABLE</text>
              <text x="182" y="125" fill="#334155" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Blue sheep, Dolphin</text>
              <text x="182" y="145" fill="#b45309" font-size="8" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Declining trend</text>

              <!-- Stage 3: Endangered -->
              <rect x="245" y="80" width="95" height="90" fill="#fed7aa" stroke="#ea580c" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/>
              <text x="292" y="105" fill="#c2410c" font-size="10.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">ENDANGERED</text>
              <text x="292" y="125" fill="#334155" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Black buck, Rhino</text>
              <text x="292" y="145" fill="#c2410c" font-size="8" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Danger of extinction</text>

              <!-- Stage 4: Extinct -->
              <rect x="355" y="80" width="80" height="90" fill="#fee2e2" stroke="#dc2626" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/>
              <text x="395" y="105" fill="#991b1b" font-size="10.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">EXTINCT</text>
              <text x="395" y="125" fill="#334155" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle">Cheetah, Duck</text>
              <text x="395" y="145" fill="#991b1b" font-size="8" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Gone forever</text>

              <!-- Arrows -->
              <path d="M 120 125 L 135 125" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="2"/>
              <path d="M 230 125 L 245 125" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="2"/>
              <path d="M 340 125 L 355 125" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="2"/>

              <rect x="40" y="195" width="380" height="30" fill="#1e293b" rx="4"/>
              <text x="230" y="215" fill="#ffffff" font-size="10.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Preventive Conservation Reverses the Risk Pathway</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">The gradual descent towards extinction when negative anthropogenic pressures go unchecked.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 2 · India's Forest Classification Breakdown</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="India Forest Land Categorization">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Reserved Forests -->
              <rect x="30" y="45" width="125" height="155" fill="#065f46" rx="6"/>
              <text x="92" y="75" fill="#ffffff" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">RESERVED</text>
              <text x="92" y="95" fill="#a7f3d0" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">> 50% of Total</text>
              <line x1="45" y1="105" x2="140" y2="105" stroke="#059669" stroke-width="1"/>
              <text x="92" y="125" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Most valuable</text>
              <text x="92" y="142" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Strictly protected</text>
              <text x="92" y="160" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">No grazing rights</text>
              <text x="92" y="180" fill="#6ee7b7" font-size="8" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Timber &amp; Wildlife</text>

              <!-- Protected Forests -->
              <rect x="167" y="45" width="125" height="155" fill="#0d9488" rx="6"/>
              <text x="230" y="75" fill="#ffffff" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">PROTECTED</text>
              <text x="230" y="95" fill="#99f6e4" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">~ 33% (1/3rd)</text>
              <line x1="182" y1="105" x2="277" y2="105" stroke="#14b8a6" stroke-width="1"/>
              <text x="230" y="125" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Protected against</text>
              <text x="230" y="142" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">further depletion</text>
              <text x="230" y="160" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Limited customary</text>
              <text x="230" y="180" fill="#5eead4" font-size="8" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">grazing allowed</text>

              <!-- Unclassed Forests -->
              <rect x="305" y="45" width="125" height="155" fill="#475569" rx="6"/>
              <text x="367" y="75" fill="#ffffff" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">UNCLASSED</text>
              <text x="367" y="95" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Remainder</text>
              <line x1="320" y1="105" x2="415" y2="105" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1"/>
              <text x="367" y="125" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Wastelands &amp; woods</text>
              <text x="367" y="142" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Owned by state &amp;</text>
              <text x="367" y="160" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">private communities</text>
              <text x="367" y="180" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="8" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Northeast &amp; Gujarat</text>

              <rect x="30" y="210" width="262" height="24" fill="#0284c7" rx="3"/>
              <text x="161" y="226" fill="#ffffff" font-size="9.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Permanent Forest Estate (Reserved + Protected = MP leads)</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Statutory classification of government-managed and community forests under Indian forestry administration.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Board Exam Quiz Section (20 Questions) -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="quiz-header">
        <div>
          <h2>CBSE Board Examination Quiz</h2>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:0;">Test your understanding of IUCN categories, Forest Categories, and Community Conservation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="quiz-score-badge" id="quizScoreBadge">Score: 0 / 20</div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-container" id="quizContainer">
        <!-- Rendered by JS -->
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
        <button type="button" class="button" id="resetQuizBtn">Reset Quiz</button>
        <span class="small" id="quizProgressText">0 of 20 answered</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Call to Action Banner -->
    <section class="cta-banner">
      <h2>Excel in CBSE Class 10 Geography</h2>
      <p>Master biodiversity classifications, national parks, wildlife sanctuaries, and project tiger with personalized expert tutoring.</p>
      <a href={calBookingUrl} class="button">Book a Doubt Clearing Session</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  const questions = [
    {
      q: "1. What is 'biodiversity' or biological diversity?",
      options: [
        "A collection of plastic toys shaped like wild animals",
        "The immense variety of living organisms in an ecosystem, bound together by complex interdependencies",
        "The count of domestic cattle in agricultural states",
        "A national chemical testing laboratory"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Biodiversity is the diversity of plant and animal species living in their natural habitat, closely integrated through ecological food webs."
    },
    {
      q: "2. Which international organization is responsible for compiling the Red Data Book classifying endangered and threatened species?",
      options: [
        "World Bank",
        "International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN)",
        "World Meteorological Organization (WMO)",
        "UNESCO World Heritage Committee"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "The IUCN (International Union for Conservation of Nature) maintains global species classification and red lists assessing extinction risk."
    },
    {
      q: "3. What are 'Endangered Species' according to IUCN?",
      options: [
        "Species that have zero population anywhere on earth",
        "Species whose populations are in danger of extinction if the negative factors causing decline continue to operate",
        "Species that live exclusively inside laboratory glass jars",
        "Species that reproduce only during leap years"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Endangered species are those whose survival is difficult if adverse factors (such as habitat destruction and poaching) continue unchecked (e.g. Indian rhino, lion-tailed macaque)."
    },
    {
      q: "4. Which of the following is classified as an 'Endemic Species'?",
      options: [
        "Nicobar pigeon and Andaman teal",
        "Sal tree and Pine tree",
        "Common dog and house cat",
        "Asiatic cheetah and Pink-headed duck"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Endemic species are confined to specific geographical pockets isolated by natural barriers, such as the Andaman wild pig, Andaman teal, and Nicobar pigeon."
    },
    {
      q: "5. When was the Asiatic Cheetah declared extinct in India?",
      options: [
        "1947",
        "1952",
        "1972",
        "2005"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "The world's fastest land mammal, the Asiatic cheetah, was officially declared extinct in India in 1952 due to hunting and habitat loss."
    },
    {
      q: "6. Which major river valley project in Madhya Pradesh submerged over 4,000 square kilometers of natural forest land?",
      options: [
        "Bhakra Nangal Dam",
        "Narmada Sagar Project",
        "Damodar Valley Corporation",
        "Hirakud Dam"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "The Narmada Sagar multi-purpose project in Madhya Pradesh inundated nearly 4,000 sq. km of pristine deciduous forest."
    },
    {
      q: "7. In which tiger reserve has ongoing dolomite mining caused severe ecological damage and disruption to elephant migration?",
      options: [
        "Corbett National Park in Uttarakhand",
        "Buxa Tiger Reserve in West Bengal",
        "Periyar Tiger Reserve in Kerala",
        "Sundarbans National Park in West Bengal"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Dolomite mining inside and around West Bengal's Buxa Tiger Reserve severely damaged natural habitat and blocked the elephant corridor."
    },
    {
      q: "8. When was the Indian Wildlife (Protection) Act implemented to provide legal protection to animal habitats?",
      options: [
        "1952",
        "1972",
        "1988",
        "2002"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "The Indian Wildlife (Protection) Act was passed in 1972, providing an all-India list of protected species and banning wildlife hunting."
    },
    {
      q: "9. When was the landmark 'Project Tiger' launched in India to protect the dwindling Bengal tiger population?",
      options: [
        "1965",
        "1973",
        "1985",
        "2000"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Project Tiger, one of the world's most comprehensive wildlife conservation ventures, was initiated in 1973."
    },
    {
      q: "10. What proportion of India's total forest area is designated as 'Reserved Forests'?",
      options: [
        "Less than 10%",
        "More than half (over 50%)",
        "Exactly 5%",
        "100%"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "More than half of the total forest land in India has been declared as Reserved Forests, regarded as the most valuable for conservation."
    },
    {
      q: "11. Which Indian state has the largest area under permanent forests (accounting for 75% of its total forest area)?",
      options: [
        "Uttar Pradesh",
        "Madhya Pradesh",
        "Rajasthan",
        "Punjab"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Madhya Pradesh holds the largest permanent forest estate in India, with reserved and protected forests covering 75% of its forest cover."
    },
    {
      q: "12. What are 'Unclassed Forests'?",
      options: [
        "Forests where no trees grow",
        "Forests and wastelands belonging to both government and private individuals/communities, widely found in the Northeast and parts of Gujarat",
        "Forests managed exclusively by the United Nations",
        "Forests planted inside university campuses"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Unclassed forests consist of tracts and scrub wastelands owned by rural village communities, private persons, and state departments."
    },
    {
      q: "13. What did the villagers of Sariska Tiger Reserve in Rajasthan do to protect their wildlife?",
      options: [
        "They demanded that the forest be cut down",
        "They fought against commercial mining citing the Wildlife Protection Act",
        "They sold the animals to private circuses",
        "They migrated to Delhi"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Villagers in Rajasthan's Alwar district actively opposed commercial dolomite mining inside Sariska by asserting the Wildlife Protection Act."
    },
    {
      q: "14. What is the 'Bhairondev Dakav Sanchuri' declared by villagers of five villages in Alwar district of Rajasthan?",
      options: [
        "A private royal palace",
        "1,200 hectares of forest designated with indigenous community rules prohibiting hunting and commercial exploitation",
        "A large copper smelting plant",
        "A municipal dump site"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "The inhabitants of 5 villages in Alwar established 1,200 hectares of forest as their own sanctuary, creating and enforcing community wildlife laws."
    },
    {
      q: "15. How did the famous 'Chipko Movement' in the Himalayas successfully prevent deforestation?",
      options: [
        "By setting the forest on fire",
        "By villagers physically hugging trees to protect them from commercial contractors' axes",
        "By building high stone walls along all valleys",
        "By importing lumber from South America"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "The Chipko Movement in Uttarakhand resisted commercial felling through villagers embracing tree trunks, demonstrating community empowerment."
    },
    {
      q: "16. What was the core message of 'Beej Bachao Andolan' in Tehri and 'Navdanya'?",
      options: [
        "That indigenous seeds and multi-crop organic farming are economically viable without using expensive synthetic chemicals",
        "That only genetic hybrid seeds should be grown",
        "That all agriculture should be stopped in the mountains",
        "That farmers should sell all farmland to real estate builders"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Beej Bachao Andolan proved that traditional crop diversity can produce healthy yields and ecological security without synthetic inputs."
    },
    {
      q: "17. Which Indian state passed the very first resolution for Joint Forest Management (JFM) in 1988?",
      options: [
        "Odisha",
        "Kerala",
        "Punjab",
        "Goa"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Odisha was the first state in India to formally pass a Joint Forest Management (JFM) resolution in 1988."
    },
    {
      q: "18. What is the foundational operational principle of Joint Forest Management (JFM)?",
      options: [
        "Local village communities form forest protection committees to manage degraded forests and share benefits like non-timber produce and timber harvests",
        "Villagers are evicted and replaced by military guards",
        "Forests are leased to foreign multinational corporations for 99 years",
        "All trees are chopped every ten years for paper mills"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "JFM integrates local village institutions into managing degraded forests in return for non-timber forest products and shared revenue from timber."
    },
    {
      q: "19. Which community in Rajasthan considers the Blackbuck (Chinkara), Nilgai, and Peacocks as sacred integral parts of their settlement?",
      options: [
        "Bishnoi community",
        "Gujjar community",
        "Gaddi shepherds",
        "Santhal tribes"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "In and around Bishnoi villages in Rajasthan, herds of blackbuck, chinkara, nilgai, and peacocks live harmoniously under community protection."
    },
    {
      q: "20. What is a 'Sacred Grove' in indigenous Indian culture?",
      options: [
        "Pristine forest patches left untouched and dedicated to folk deities, protected by tribal customs and taboos against any cutting",
        "An artificial garden inside a luxury hotel",
        "A greenhouse for growing ornamental roses",
        "A government timber nursery"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Sacred groves (deorais) are patches of primeval forest conserved by tribal communities out of reverence to nature gods, untouched by commercial felling."
    }
  ];

  function renderQuiz() {
    const container = document.getElementById('quizContainer');
    if (!container) return;
    container.innerHTML = '';

    questions.forEach((item, idx) => {
      const qCard = document.createElement('div');
      qCard.className = 'quiz-card';
      qCard.id = `qCard_${idx}`;

      const title = document.createElement('h3');
      title.className = 'quiz-question';
      title.textContent = item.q;
      qCard.appendChild(title);

      const optList = document.createElement('div');
      optList.className = 'quiz-options';

      item.options.forEach((optText, optIdx) => {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        btn.className = 'quiz-opt-btn';
        btn.textContent = optText;
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => handleSelectOption(idx, optIdx));
        optList.appendChild(btn);
      });
      qCard.appendChild(optList);

      const feedback = document.createElement('div');
      feedback.className = 'quiz-feedback';
      feedback.id = `feedback_${idx}`;
      qCard.appendChild(feedback);

      container.appendChild(qCard);
    });
  }

  let userAnswers = {};

  function handleSelectOption(qIdx, optIdx) {
    if (userAnswers[qIdx] !== undefined) return;
    userAnswers[qIdx] = optIdx;

    const q = questions[qIdx];
    const isCorrect = (optIdx === q.correct);
    const card = document.getElementById(`qCard_${qIdx}`);
    const btns = card.querySelectorAll('.quiz-opt-btn');
    const fb = document.getElementById(`feedback_${qIdx}`);

    btns.forEach((btn, i) => {
      btn.disabled = true;
      if (i === q.correct) {
        btn.classList.add('opt-correct');
      } else if (i === optIdx) {
        btn.classList.add('opt-wrong');
      }
    });

    if (isCorrect) {
      fb.className = 'quiz-feedback feedback-correct';
      fb.innerHTML = `<strong>Correct!</strong> ${q.explanation}`;
    } else {
      fb.className = 'quiz-feedback feedback-wrong';
      fb.innerHTML = `<strong>Incorrect.</strong> ${q.explanation}`;
    }

    updateScoreSummary();
  }

  function updateScoreSummary() {
    let score = 0;
    const answeredCount = Object.keys(userAnswers).length;
    for (let qIdx in userAnswers) {
      if (userAnswers[qIdx] === questions[qIdx].correct) {
        score++;
      }
    }
    const badge = document.getElementById('quizScoreBadge');
    if (badge) badge.textContent = `Score: ${score} / ${questions.length}`;

    const prog = document.getElementById('quizProgressText');
    if (prog) prog.textContent = `${answeredCount} of ${questions.length} answered`;
  }

  function resetQuiz() {
    userAnswers = {};
    renderQuiz();
    updateScoreSummary();
  }

  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    renderQuiz();
    const resetBtn = document.getElementById('resetQuizBtn');
